#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171738 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5c8e2ab-4da7-5e43-a61f-b1dbf76d99ef" :knowledge-point/title "Raising Numbers to Larger Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "9bdfdfa3-5ed2-5cf9-bf8b-ee84bf4bb9c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14474" :question/problem "Evaluate $7^4$." :question/worked-solution "According to the definition,\n$4$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n7 \\times 7 = 49\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\times 7}_{\\color{blue}49} \\times \\underbrace{7 \\times 7}_{\\color{blue}49} = {\\color{blue}49} \\times {\\color{blue}49}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $49 \\times 49$.\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}3[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}8[/math]; 4\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ + & 1 & 9\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 0 \\mid 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7^4=2,401$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171739 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ccf09f0-c76d-5133-be05-1fdfa61b23ad" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Two-Digit Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "53a23554-58d8-595a-8e14-bd36e00f678d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14476" :question/problem "Evaluate $31^2$." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate $31^2$, we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n$2$\n\nThen, we work out $31 \\times 31$.\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}1; 3\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n31 \\times 31 = 961\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171759 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5178592b-14ed-520a-a375-e95219ba8fa3" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Numbers Ending in Zeros"}] :question/id #uuid "60c9af0c-e7f2-5027-855b-dcc5868e3ee3" :question/math-academy-id "e-15038" :question/problem "Evaluate $220^2$." :question/worked-solution "To evaluate $220^2$, we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n$2$\n\nThen, we work out $220 \\times 220$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}220 & ⟶220 \\\\ 220 & ⟶220\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 \\\\ + & 4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}0; 4 \\mid 8 \\mid 4\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}484}{\\color{blue}00}= 48,400\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n220^2 = 48,400\n$$"}]}
